inished_at":"2026-11-27T12:41:43.238361+00:00","id":"70cb436b4ef3eb5a4bad","operation":"governed_repository_head","outcome":"completed","target":"grandchallenge/MATHSOLVE"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:41:48.274536+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:02.803723+00:00","id":"0f5936193d3a2a2300e7","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:10.057736+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:13.763404+00:00","id":"4c3bf396419404d0c46b","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:14.913420+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:18.991794+00:00","id":"a15db5bc426659767b28","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:27.238013+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:30.269616+00:00","id":"ced225de662b35fce98e","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:38.905285+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:39.949983+00:00","id":"a9b6cd328c0fb4fd5367","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:41.120309+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:42.639031+00:00","id":"a3d71fd37900f1650115","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:52.360024+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:58.114504+00:00","id":"9e2fcc035bf6b2322f9c","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:05.732219+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:08.087772+00:00","id":"9b28de8b05b905256b01","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:09.279257+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:09.834186+00:00","id":"531b921d71cc04777cbe","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:14.862405+00:00","exit_code":null,"finished_at":null,"id":"1b9e0b7e1e24b8f4ea24","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"accepted","target":"CHAD Bash"}],"repository_evidence":[{"blob_sha":"2bf5d5b57125f0a0d31134d0a91519bd5a7bf336","committed_at":"2026-11-27T11:15:59+00:00","end_line":979,"excerpt":"## 25. Why every fixed height moment becomes finite in large dimension\n\nThe partition construction has a dimension-independent growth exponent. But the differential forms constructed from it may still grow as a power of height when their arithmetic geometry becomes thin. The next theorem, SMom, must prove that this growth is integrable. The source establishes a concrete sufficient threshold: for a nonnegative integer moment order $A$, every matrix dimension $N\\geq A+2$ admits a finite-volume arithmetic fundamental domain with finite integral of the height power of order $A$.\n\n### Reducing an unbounded geometry to diagonal coordinates\n\nThe source first makes a rational change of basis in the adelic group, which describes the number field at its infinite and finite places together. The reduced basis is built by choosing a rational row of least adelic height and applying the same construction to the remaining block. The minimum exists because a bounded-height family of rational lines has finitely many representatives up to rational scaling. This follows using the product formula, discreteness of rational vectors, and compact representatives of idele classes in a bounded positive interval of modules.\n\nA two-row approximation supplies the crucial inequality between adjacent diagonal entries. The source uses a pigeonhole argument in the coefficient box of an integral basis: more candidate integral multiples than small subboxes force two multiples to have close fractional coordinates. Their difference gives a nonzero rational row of controlled norm. Applied to the last two rows of a triangular matrix, the approximation bounds their diagonal ratio because the last row was chosen to have minimal height.\n\n","id":"0bae9021ac91f13775b0","observed_at":"2026-11-27T12:42:57.386659+00:00","path":"docs/domains/rh_a26_audit_explained.md","policy":"GCL_PUBLIC_REPOSITORY_EVIDENCE/1","redactions":{},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME","revision":"e619126c0a89aa86da72f9c36c9247c16c0b8b98","source_sha256":"a254223f57d63ecbc7c6e74753937a2663d9a9487f0bb3d900c00274ed5be759","start_line":970,"total_lines":1131},{"blob_sha":"9fcf5231bcc81501306cffa082b60fcb68d6117f","committed_at":"2026-11-27T09:26:39+00:00","end_line":24,"excerpt":"# SCC-001: Target-relative gluing and compositional transport\n\nStatus: exploratory author-derived propositions, bounded exact finite replay, no formal certification or novelty claim. Continuation of [SCC-001](https://github.com/grandchallenge/MATHSOLVE/issues/1309) and [syzygy transport](SYZYGY_TRANSPORT.md). Public [illustrated chapter](https://grandchallenge.ai/MATH-PROGRAMME/scc/).\n\n## 1. Mark a target, rather than a gate\n\nLet B be the Boolean function algebra over F2, V contain constants and coordinates, and F_r be the predecessor filtration. Fix a single Boolean decision function f. Let B_0 and B_1 be the two support ideals determined by f=0 and f=1. Define N_r=F_r+fF_r, and the target-relative gluing space G_r=N_r/F_r. Equivalently, N_r is the direct sum of the two independent restrictions (embedded by zero extension) of F_r to the target's zero and one regions. This follows because (1+f)a=a+fa. Thus G_r measures the gap between independent realizability in the two regions and joint realizability by a single expression in F_r.\n\nWriting d_r=dim G_r,\n\n$$\nd_r=\\dim(fF_r)+\\dim((1+f)F_r)-\\dim F_r.\n$$\n\nIt follows that d_r is nonnegative and at most the smaller of the two restriction dimensions, hence at most the smaller support-cell size. The marked class f+F_r is zero precisely when f belongs to F_r. Unlike the whole G_r, this marked class detects target completion at r=1.\n\n## 2. A common algebra for successive gates\n\nThe Rees algebra R=sum over r>=0 of F_r z^r is a graded subalgebra of B[z]; z is formal and represents a one-step increase of allowed degree. Put N=R+fR and G=N/R. As a graded R-module,\n\n$$\nG\\cong R/J_f,\\qquad J_f=\\{a\\in R:fa\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\n","id":"91caa4c0b2e9739f7a4f","observed_at":"2026-11-27T12:43:02.873267+00:00","path":"research/SCC-001/TARGET_GLUE.md","policy":"GCL_PUBLIC_REPOSITORY_EVIDENCE/1","redactions":{},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATHSOLVE","revision":"33cba0ffcb221b04b540753ee585f8b96a97c7df","source_sha256":"ec41ad8008df7b6d2bb011bd3a14076e5f154519b9c7a8a76ff70728dbad786f","start_line":1,"total_lines":71}],"retention_limit":48,"schema":"GCL_CONTROL_ACTIVITY_FEED/2","transcripts":[{"command":"git -C ~/MATH-PROGRAMME show 983e98f7971b67623461149068091e91e1a22b06:governance/erdos_proof_verification/evidence/0562/B_FIRST_MOMENT_FIXTURES_001.py | head -n 120","exit_code":0,"id":"d81134fc758ef8280499","policy":"GCL_AUTOMATIC_PUBLIC_SOURCE_TRANSCRIPT/1","redactions":{"command":{},"output":{}},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME","revision":"983e98f7971b67623461149068091e91e1a22b06","source_end":"2026-11-27T12:24:05.466372+00:00","stdout_stderr":"\"\"\"Exact finite adversarial fixtures; not a proof of the unbounded theorem.\"\"\"\nfrom itertools import product\nfrom math import comb\n\ndef root12_floor(x):\n    lo, hi = 0, 1 << ((x.bit_length()+11)//12)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo+hi+1)//2\n        if mid**12 <= x: lo = mid\n        else: hi = mid-1\n    return lo\n\ndef main():\n    masks=0\n    for t in range(7):\n        for mask in product((None,False,True), repeat=t):\n            count=sum(all(m is None or m==b for m,b in zip(mask,bits)) for bits in product((False,True),repeat=t))\n            assert count==2**mask.count(None)\n            masks+=1\n    for n in range(4,129):\n        a=(n-1)*(n-2); k=comb(n,3); m=root12_fl